speechtext:
Mr. Chairman, coming as I do from one of the constituencies that is *within the boundaries of the great port and *city of Montreal, I have some interest in this bill. The second half of my lifetime I have lived in that great inland city. The first half of my life, though, I lived on the Atlantic ocean, and I myself, if you will allow a personal reference, have been a member of the bar for some 33 years, in the civil law and in the common law. I have read this bill twice over, myself. I understand it. With whatever due modesty I can command, as a member of the legal profession, I understand the bill.
My hon. friend who just spoke referred twice to some poetry, and had a little difficulty quoting it. After having what I believe to be a complete comprehension of this bill I say to all:
A little learning is a dangerous thing;
Drink deep, or taste not the Pierian spring;
